ABSTRACT
This study offers a comprehensive exploration of the cooling strategies and power ratings of electric vehicle motors. Electric vehicles depend on robust electric motors for propulsion. High power and sustained high-speed usage create excessive heat, risking motor failure without effective thermal management. Implementation of effective cooling strategies thus becomes imperative to ensure both the longevity and optimal performance of electric vehicle motors. This article seeks to review range of cooling approaches, encompassing air cooling, liquid cooling, and hybrid cooling, scrutinizing their respective capabilities in efficiently dissipating the heat generated during motor operation. Furthermore, the study investigates the impact of power ratings on the performance of electric vehicle motors in determining its maximum power output with higher ratings. In summary, this article underscores the critical importance of carefully selecting the appropriate cooling strategy and power rating for electric vehicle motors to maximize their efficiency and durability. This insight proves to be invaluable to electric vehicle manufacturers and designers, providing them with essential guidance in the design and selection of electric vehicle motors that meet the demands of modern transportation while contributing to a sustainable future.
1. Introduction
The increase in the use of fossil fuels has significantly contributed to the growing concerns about anthropogenic climate change. Transportation is a significant cause for this climate change as it accounts for approximately 24 % of the global energy-related CO2 emission [1–4]. Therefore, implementing an active measure for reducing this CO2 emission in the transportation sector is the need of the hour. In response to this challenge, governments and major transportation sectors have turned towards electric vehicles (EVs) as one of the long-term solutions to reduce CO2 emissions [5,6]. Adding value to this point, Fig. 1 shows the year-wise growth of EVs in the global market from 2010 to 2022 and
the EVs usage is expected to reach 230 million by 2030 [7–9]. Hence, the production of EVs has been accelerating rapidly over the recent years and has now become a promising step towards achieving a sustainable and eco-friendly environment. However, the performance reliability of EVs is also an important factor in evaluating their suitability for widespread adoption [10]. The reliability and lifespan of an EV depend on several critical factors including the motor, battery pack, controllers, and thermal management systems. Fig. 2 schematises the typical sectional view of EV [11]. Due to the technological advancement in EVs, the size of the battery pack and the motor has been reduced, resulting in an increased power density and local hotspot formation. These hotspots can cause heat transportation problems, such as reducing the efficiency of the motor and battery,
