bars, which can lead to thermo-mechanical failure. Existing findings [19,20] indicate that, about 75 % of motor performance issues are localised to the stator coil winding and rotor bars, which are affected by temperature change and mechanical stress. Therefore, in addition to EV battery packs, reducing the motor’s hotspot formation is essential to improve the EV’s performance reliability. An effective thermal management strategy for both components can ensure reliable, efficient and durable EVs, making them a suitable alternative to traditional gasoline-powered vehicles. Further, the studies on the motor power rating and the technologies used, helps determining the prospect and the direction of research in the thermal management of EV motors. The comprehensive review of the performance of electric vehicle (EV) motors underscores the necessity for thermal management strategies. These are crucial in ensuring the reliable performance and extended lifespan of EV motors. This research comprehensively analyses different motor technologies and their thermal management strategies used in EVs. The study highlights the novelty of analysing the significance of the design parameters (cooling channel’s position/design, radial fins, cooling fan design), thermophysical properties of coolants and heat transfer enhancers (phase-change materials, thermal fillers and heat pipes (HPs)) in the effective cooling of the motor. The research also emphasises the significance of computation-based modelling in predicting motor heat loss and temperature for reliable motor performance. Thus, the study sets the base for the research community in selecting the appropriate cooling systems for the optimised motor performance. Adopting the advanced cooling strategy helps manufacturers to reduce energy consumption, emission and develop advanced industrial standards to make EVs a reliable and sustainable transportation solution. The advanced industrial standards thus developed can contribute to the clean energy and climate action of the environmental, social, and governance framework and UN sustainable development goals (SDGs). The research on motors cooling strategies and power rating is organised in the following structure. It begins with an introduction that sets the context and highlights the importance of efficient cooling systems in EV motors. The second section explores EV motor technologies and discusses heat loss. Section 3 discusses various cooling and heat transfer enhancement techniques adopted in EV motors. The content also addresses computation-based numerical modelling of the motor for heat transfer and predicting the thermal values. The contents follow the discussion over the possible advancement in motor cooling in the future in section 5 and present the concluding remarks on the key findings under section 6.
