3.1.3. Effect of cooling fan design
In the air-cooling approach, the most convenient way of creating forced air circulation is by attaching the fans to the motor spindle. The study of Nikitina [91] on air-cooled motors revealed that, the temperature drop is directly proportional to the volumetric airflow. In another work, Li [92] studied the air cooling of PM motors with a centrifugal impeller attached to the rotor core and observed a linear correlation between airflow rate and motor rpm. The studies on armature heat transfer analysis revealed a gradual temperature rise along the axial direction, highlighting the need for design changes in impeller and diffuser, to reduce the motor load and flow losses. Kim et al. [93] attached combinations of air gap fans along the rotor and numerically studied its cooling performance on a 100 kW AC-IM. The results revealed that, the air gap fan increased the air circulation and reduced the maximum temperature of the rotor and stator windings from 324 and 143 ◦C to 245 and 121 ◦C, respectively. Further, the winding cooling performance index increases by 35, 36 and 55 %, for the air gap fan attached along the front, rear and two sides of the rotor windings, respectively. Fawzal et al. [94] attached fans to the backside of the rotor in 100 kW AFPM. The research focused on the winding losses and rotor cooling performance index for backwards-inclined, radial and teardrop pillar blade designs using CFD software. The results showed that, the backwards-inclined airfoil blade design had relatively higher rotor cooling performance due to the lower winding losses when compared to radial and pillar blade designs. Roffi et al. [95] studied the possibility of replacing the centrifugal impeller with the axial flow impeller to reduce the noise and improve overall motor efficiency. The study involves analysing four designs of
