3.2.2. Effect of cooling channel design
In addition to the coolant flow rate and temperature, the design of the cooling channel is also a critical factor in achieving efficient coolant distribution and effective motor cooling. The cooling channel is responsible for transporting liquid coolant into and out of the motor to absorb the heat generated by direct or indirect cooling. As a result, several studies have focussed on enhancing the motor’s cooling performance by modifying the cooling channel design. The study investigates various aspects of the channel’s geometry, such as its cross-sectional shape, size, and orientation, to determine their impact on coolant flow and heat transfer characteristics. The studies seek to identify the most effective cooling channel design parameters for a given motor configuration and operating conditions, ultimately improving the motor’s cooling efficiency. Indirect cooling is preferred in the liquid cooling system using water as the coolant, as it is a universal solvent that dissolves the impurities and becomes electrically conductive. Fig. 9 outlines the schematics of the most common indirect cooling channel designs. Sikora et al. [111] embedded the cooling water pipes along the stator SAVH of the stator core and reduced the motor’s rotor temperature from 112 to 84 ◦C. Pecha ́nek and Bouzek [112] analysed the axial and tangential cooling channel duct design and, observed a higher heat transfer rate and reduced motor temperature for the axial duct design than the tangential flow duct design. Chiu et al. [86] attached the radial fins to the outer side of axial flow cooling duct casing and observed a 1.8 ◦C temperature drop highlighting convective heat transfer rate of water is more significant than the heat transfer by conductive fins. Rhebergen et al. [113] developed a unique polymer duct from the Elan-Cast mixture containing 10 % aluminium nitride and 10 % boron nitride and axially mounted it inside the stator core. The axial cooling duct reduced the motor’s
average temperature by 65 and 90 ◦C on the stator and rotor surface, respectively. Li et al. [114] numerically studied and optimised the cooling channel design parameters, such as the cooling channel’s width, height and adjacent width. The study reported an optimum cooling channel width of 10–16 mm and a 5 mm channel height for maximum cooling performance. Rehman and Seong [102] analysed the cooling performance of the radial cooling jacket by varying the number of passes and ports. It was reported that the cooling jacket design with two ports and six passes or above could maintain the motor temperature below the critical temperature limit. Bedkowshi et al. [115] numerically modelled and analysed an in-wheel motor cooling system’s stator support structures and coolant flow pattern design to enhance the heat transfer rate. The work involves introducing L-shaped aluminium radiators in the place of thermally conductive resin, observing a maximum temperature drop of 17.2 ◦C. Deriszadeh et al. [103,104] analysed the spiral cooling channel design, as shown in Fig. 9(c) with a different number of turns (2 to 8) to remove the heat from the motor. The results reported that the eight-turn spiral cooling jacket has maximum cooling performance regardless of the coolant (aluminium oxide nanofluid and water ethylene glycol mixtures). McCluskey et al. [116] designed a microchannel-based heat exchanger for cooling a 200 kW motor’s stator. The results indicate that the microchannel integrated cooling methods can fit aircraft propulsion motors of 1 MW capacity. Tikadar et al. [117] introduced rectangular, circular and direct winding heat exchanger (DWHX) cooling channel designs along the stator core to reduce thermal resistance, as shown in Fig. 10. The studies on the design’s cooling effect and magnetic flux interference revealed that the DWHX design has minimum magnetic flux interference and offers superior cooling of 88 to 120 ◦C compared to 14
