3.3.2. Thermal filler materials
Thermal fillers or potting materials are substances used to fill the air gap between end windings and the casing. These materials facilitate an efficient thermal transfer path, enhancing heat dissipation in the system. Yao et al. [140] analysed the effect of using the Aluminium nitride potting material in 7 kW PMSM and observed a 20.3 ◦C drop in motor temperature. Polikarpova et al. [141] studied heat transfer enhancement in a 100 kV A AFPM motor using ceramacast 675-N as potting material. The results revealed a 17 ◦C temperature drop along stator end windings. Polikarpova et al. [142], in a comparative study of ceramacast 675-N and epoxy 2315 as potting material in the stator, reported a maximum temperature deviation of 2 ◦C due to their difference in thermal conductivity. Melka et al. [90] covered the stator core with the potting thermal fillers, as shown in Fig. 13(a). The studies on the motor temperature rise with plain and finned casing revealed a temperature drop of 18.5 and 29.8 ◦C, respectively. Sun et al. [109] experimented with potting
silicone gelatine material in a PMSM motor, as shown in Fig. 13(b). The analysis revealed a 27.3 ◦C temperature drop and doubled peak load operation time. Chai et al. [143] introduced an inner bulge along the stator core of the AFPM motor and impregnated the stator windings with epoxy resins. The results revealed that the motor temperature drop is a linear function of epoxy resin’s thermal conductivity and showed a temperature drop of 8.3 ◦C when using resin with a thermal conductivity of 0.8 W/mK. McCluskey et al. [116] study on the potting material’s thermal conductivity revealed that a rise in thermal conductivity considerably reduces the maximum winding temperature. Li et al. [144] analysis on potting the external rotor PM motor end-windings air gap with thermal grease reduced the end-windings temperature by 74 ◦C and increased the maximum torque from 92 to 115.2Nm. Table 6 summarises the winding temperature data observed by researchers while using the thermal fillers of different thermal conductivity in the motor.
3.3.3. Embedded conductive materials
The incorporation of conductive material along the motor core and stator teeth serves to reduce heat transfer resistance. This optimization enhances the efficiency of the cooling system by facilitating a more effective heat transfer path. Galea et al. [145] inserted T-shaped copper as a heat path in the stator winding and reduced the maximum temperature from 180 to 112 ◦C. Polikarpova et al. [146] introduced copper bars along the stator of 75 kW AFPM and achieved a 25 ◦C temperature drop. Pyrh ̈onen et al. [147] inserted a series of copper rods into the stator core of 75 kW AFPM on both potted and unpotted stators. The studies showed a temperature drop of 16 and 26 ◦C along the unpotted stator and 14 and 26 ◦C for the potted stator with single and three copper rods, respectively. Huynh and Hsieh [148] modified the copper windings insulation with materials of higher thermal conductivity and observed that the heat transfer rate increases with the thermal conductance of the insulator. Table 7 summarises the temperature data observed by researchers by adding embedded conductive materials in motors.
3.3.4. Heat transfer enhancement using heat pipes
Heat pipes (HPs) are passive heat transfer devices exhibiting exceptionally high thermal conductivity. They can be strategically positioned along the motor windings and core to facilitate enhanced cooling. By utilising HPs, heat generated within the motor can be efficiently transferred from the evaporative end to the condensing end. This enables effective heat dissipation and temperature regulation, contributing to improved motor performance and reliability. The high thermal conductivity of HPs allows for efficient heat transfer even over long
