3.3.5. Inferences on heat transfer enhancement techniques
Studies on heat transfer enhancement techniques in electric vehicle motors have revealed a significant improvement in the performance of the motor’s cooling system. This leads to more stable and reliable motor operation.The increased temperature drop and reduced heat transfer resistance, highlight the improved cooling system efficiency. Further, the studies on thermal enhancers show the possibility of overloaded operation within the operating temperature, which can be unfavourable to the motor’s performance as mechanical wear increases rapidly. Therefore, it is crucial to ensure that the thermal enhancing systems should be designed and implemented to balance the need for improved cooling and the risk of overloading. The studies also signify that hybridising the cooling system reduces the energy spent on the coolant circulation, especially with air-cooled HPs. However, the use of high heat flux HPs in electric vehicle motor thermal management is still limited, suggesting that there is room for further research to improve the performance and efficiency of electric vehicle motors.
4. Computational models for temperature control of EV motors
Computational modelling and temperature control involve predicting the thermal behaviour of electric motors using numerical models such as LPTN, FEA, and CFD and optimising the motor’s temperature values for improved performance and reliability. Numerical models simulate diverse operating conditions, offering a comprehensive view of a motor’s thermal behavior.This prediction method allows for
identifying hot spots, thereby optimising the cooling system to prevent overheat. The following content summarises the existing studies on developing EV motor numerical models and real-time predictive temperature control systems. Huai et al. [175] modelled the heat transfer and thermal losses of the enclosed air-cooled AC-IM with an error of less than 10 %. Howey et al. [176,177] simulated the fluid domain in the AFPM motor to calculate the convective heat transfer coefficient value. The results highlight rotor surface roughness and rotational Re are the major influential parameters. Wrobel et al. [178] developed an FEA-based thermal model incorporating the motor losses and calculated the thermal value of segmented stator winding with an error of less than 9 %. Wrobel et al. [179] also studied the loss component of the AFPM motor’s rotor using CFD and 3D LPTN models. The predicted CFD and 3D LPTN model losses are close to the experimental value. Ponomarev et al. [180] studied the effectiveness and reliability of direct oil-cooled PMSM obtained using the LPTN, FEM and CFD methods and stated that the CFD method produces more reliable thermal values due to its ability to simulate complex fluid flow and heat transfer phenomena. The study highlights motor core loss is a function of magnetic flux and frequency. Grabowski et al. [181] numerically simulated the temperature distribution of the AC-IM fins using COMSOL software with an error of less than 10 %. The temperature distribution along the motor cooling fins is the function of the material’s thermophysical properties, such as specific heat and thermal conductivity. Nikitina [91] numerically modelled the heat generation and studied the thermal values in the motor spindle.
