Rasekh et al. [182] computationally simulated and correlated the heat transfer coefficient for an AFPM synchronous machine with an error of less than 5 % using magnet parameters like rotational Re, magnet thickness ratio and magnet angle ratio. Sheela and Mohan [183] modelled a 3 kW PMSM using the FEA-based magnetic software, which produced accurate results when verified with 3 kW PMSM. Grunditz et al. [184] modelled the thermal values and heat transfer resistance of PMSM motors of different stator core slot thicknesses using the LPTN model. The studies revealed that the slot thickness affects the motor’s copper and iron losses, and a larger slot thickness doubles the peak load operation time of the original motor. Zhu et al. [79] proposed a double-loop circulatory calculation technique to reduce the thermal model complexity and achieved an error percentage of less than 5 %. Wei et al. [185] developed a one-dimensional model of PMSM and optimised the motor parameters using the MIGA-NLPQL combination optimisation algorithm. The studies reduced motor loss by 18.35 %, increasing driving range by 3.4 %. Yang et al. [186] proposed a loss minimisation method based on the variable voltage control strategy to improve the motor and inverter efficiency, as shown in Fig. 16. The study enhanced the motor and inverter efficiency by 0.77 and 12.8 %, respectively, for stable drive system operation. Braut et al. [187] analysis of the fatigue life of the PMSM rotor using transient thermo-mechanical 3D FEM predicted that the rotor magnet pocket upper filet is the critical stress region prone to failure. Ganji et al. [188] modelled and simulated the performance of an 8/6 SRM generator (SRG) using the 2D FEM and proposed the necessity of a control system based on the numerical model. The research further analysed the motor iron losses for each time step and observed magnetic flux density and frequency as influential parameters. In monitoring the heat generation and dissipation of the motors, the condition monitoring system collects motor data like temperature, speed, vibration, dielectric spectroscopy, and line leakage current and predicts the failure based on the data [189]. Shu et al. [190] analysed the failure of the motor control components using the fault tree diagnostic techniques to predict the reliability of the EV motor controller.
The study reports that surface-mounted components of the controller are prone to failure due to the motor temperature and require periodic replacements for reliable operation. Demetriades et al. [191] developed a real-time thermal model of PM motors and implemented it in a DSP to predict the thermal values of the motor in real-time. The study generates the motor loss values numerically and uses them as model input for the control system. The model-generated thermal values are closer to the experimental result observed. Dong et al. [68,192] further examined the motor’s thermal values using the motor loss models and attempted to correct the temperature values based on the real-time thermocouple data input. The real-time data reduced the model’s error percentage up to 85 %. Huang et al. [166] used a real-time motor temperature prediction-based cooling system controller for a hybrid HP-enhanced water-cooled PM motor. The proposed model assesses the heat generated and the energy consumption for the motor cooling system, such as pump, fan and operates coolant pump for optimum coolant flow, thus reducing the cooling system energy consumption by 57 %. Table 9 summarises the numerical model and the correlations for the prediction of motor heat loss and heat transfer parameters developed by the researchers. The observations from the prior works on the computational modelling of electric motors reveal the potential for reliable prediction of the motor’s thermal values. These models can help optimise the design of the motor for better performance and longer lifespan, as well as improve the overall efficiency of the cooling system. Integrating these numerical models with real-time condition monitoring systems makes it possible to accurately predict the motor’s temperature and adjust the cooling system’s energy consumption. Continued research and development could lead to more accurate models predicting the motor’s thermal behaviour with relatively lesser uncertainty under a wider range of operating conditions. In addition, the predictive thermal management techniques integrated with artificial intelligence can be even more efficient and will be worth initiative to provide real-time predictions and recommendations based on the motor’s operating conditions.
